\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The reserved notation at the tetrahedron of Example\nonbreakingspace \ref {ex:tetra}. Left: the design, with $C=\{0,1,2\}$ the shaded face, $g_3$ the omitted atom, and the chart atom $v_c=\sqrt  {t_c}\,g_c$ marked at half length on each arrow, where $\lVert v_c\rVert ^2=s_c$. Upper right: the two products of $M_C$ of \eqref  {eq:twoproducts}, different matrices of one trace, one determinant and one spectrum; the equivalence below them is Lemma\nonbreakingspace \ref {lem:flip} with Lemma\nonbreakingspace \ref {lem:chart}, and holds at the one selection size $\lvert C\rvert =k$ at which $M_C$ is square. Bottom: the chart of \eqref  {eq:chart}, with the block $W[C]$ shaded; its determinant is taken directly and again through Corollary\nonbreakingspace \ref {cor:minor}, and the same reading at the pair $\{0,1\}$ returns $\tfrac  3{16}$, so the $3\times 3$ minor vanishes and the $2\times 2$ one does not. The strip on the right places the design at $m=4$ against the window $N(3)=7$ and rings the two cells of Corollary\nonbreakingspace \ref {cor:twocells}. Every weight, leverage and leverage score is constant in $c$ here, so the plate carries no variation across the atoms and no domination with positive slack.}}{8}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:notation}{{3}{8}{The reserved notation at the tetrahedron of Example~\ref {ex:tetra}. Left: the design, with $C=\{0,1,2\}$ the shaded face, $g_3$ the omitted atom, and the chart atom $v_c=\sqrt {t_c}\,g_c$ marked at half length on each arrow, where $\lVert v_c\rVert ^2=s_c$. Upper right: the two products of $M_C$ of \eqref {eq:twoproducts}, different matrices of one trace, one determinant and one spectrum; the equivalence below them is Lemma~\ref {lem:flip} with Lemma~\ref {lem:chart}, and holds at the one selection size $\lvert C\rvert =k$ at which $M_C$ is square. Bottom: the chart of \eqref {eq:chart}, with the block $W[C]$ shaded; its determinant is taken directly and again through Corollary~\ref {cor:minor}, and the same reading at the pair $\{0,1\}$ returns $\tfrac 3{16}$, so the $3\times 3$ minor vanishes and the $2\times 2$ one does not. The strip on the right places the design at $m=4$ against the window $\win (3)=7$ and rings the two cells of Corollary~\ref {cor:twocells}. Every weight, leverage and leverage score is constant in $c$ here, so the plate carries no variation across the atoms and no domination with positive slack}{figure.3}{}}
