\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces The design \eqref  {eq:sic} on the Bloch sphere. A line in $\mathbb  {C}^{2}$ is a point of $S^{2}$, and unit vectors obey $\lvert \langle u,v\rangle \rvert ^{2}=\tfrac  12\bigl  (1+n_u\cdot n_v\bigr  )$. At leverage $2$ the value $\tfrac  43$ of Example\nonbreakingspace \ref {ex:sic} therefore reads $n_a\cdot n_b=-\tfrac  13$, and the four Bloch vectors are the vertices of a regular tetrahedron: all six pairs are congruent, so all six share the characteristic polynomial of Lemma\nonbreakingspace \ref {lem:sicvalue}. At $k=2$ the argument of $\langle g_a,g_b\rangle \langle g_b,g_c\rangle \langle g_c,g_a\rangle $ is half the oriented area of the geodesic triangle $n_a n_b n_c$; the hatched face has area $\pi $, so that product is purely imaginary. Over $\mathbb  {R}$ the Bloch map is the angle-doubling map \eqref  {eq:doubleangle} normalized, and its image is a great circle. Three points of a circle bound no area, which is the phase defect of Remark\nonbreakingspace \ref {rem:phase} vanishing over $\mathbb  {R}$.}}{30}{figure.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bloch}{{15}{30}{The design \eqref {eq:sic} on the Bloch sphere. A line in $\C ^{2}$ is a point of $S^{2}$, and unit vectors obey $\lvert \langle u,v\rangle \rvert ^{2}=\tfrac 12\bigl (1+n_u\cdot n_v\bigr )$. At leverage $2$ the value $\tfrac 43$ of Example~\ref {ex:sic} therefore reads $n_a\cdot n_b=-\tfrac 13$, and the four Bloch vectors are the vertices of a regular tetrahedron: all six pairs are congruent, so all six share the characteristic polynomial of Lemma~\ref {lem:sicvalue}. At $k=2$ the argument of $\langle g_a,g_b\rangle \langle g_b,g_c\rangle \langle g_c,g_a\rangle $ is half the oriented area of the geodesic triangle $n_a n_b n_c$; the hatched face has area $\pi $, so that product is purely imaginary. Over $\R $ the Bloch map is the angle-doubling map \eqref {eq:doubleangle} normalized, and its image is a great circle. Three points of a circle bound no area, which is the phase defect of Remark~\ref {rem:phase} vanishing over $\R $}{figure.15}{}}
