\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces The two operations of \S  \ref {sec:cpadding}, and the ladder they compose. Above: the matrix $A$ of a design has $c$-th row $g_c^{\mathsf  {H}}$ and satisfies $A^{\mathsf  {H}}DA=I_k$; the spike borders it with one column and one row, the split repeats one row, and in each case the bordered Parseval product is the identity of the new rank. The spike divides the value bound by $1-w$ and the split leaves it alone, which is the whole cost accounting of Theorem\nonbreakingspace \ref {thm:ladder}. Below: the cells $(m,k)$ for $2\le k\le 6$, the shaded band $m\le k+1$ where $\mathrm  {GTZ}_{\mathbb  {C}}$ holds by Proposition\nonbreakingspace \ref {prop:cpositive}, and the staircase at $(9,5)$: three spikes up the diagonal $m=k+2$, then two splits along $k=5$. The tabulated $w=1-(\tfrac  9{10})^{1/e}$ gives $(1-w)^{e}=\tfrac  9{10}$ at every rank, so the bound after the spikes is $\tfrac  {10}9\alpha _{\mathrm  {SIC}}$ whatever $k$ is, and since $b<b/(1-w)$ for every $b>0$ and every admissible $w$ no member of the family attains $b$.}}{32}{figure.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:padding}{{16}{32}{The two operations of \S \ref {sec:cpadding}, and the ladder they compose. Above: the matrix $A$ of a design has $c$-th row $g_c\adj $ and satisfies $A\adj DA=I_k$; the spike borders it with one column and one row, the split repeats one row, and in each case the bordered Parseval product is the identity of the new rank. The spike divides the value bound by $1-w$ and the split leaves it alone, which is the whole cost accounting of Theorem~\ref {thm:ladder}. Below: the cells $(m,k)$ for $2\le k\le 6$, the shaded band $m\le k+1$ where $\GtzC $ holds by Proposition~\ref {prop:cpositive}, and the staircase at $(9,5)$: three spikes up the diagonal $m=k+2$, then two splits along $k=5$. The tabulated $w=1-(\tfrac 9{10})^{1/e}$ gives $(1-w)^{e}=\tfrac 9{10}$ at every rank, so the bound after the spikes is $\tfrac {10}9\alpha _{\mathrm {SIC}}$ whatever $k$ is, and since $b<b/(1-w)$ for every $b>0$ and every admissible $w$ no member of the family attains $b$}{figure.16}{}}
