\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Identity \eqref  {eq:tripledet} at $s=1$, where it reads $\det (S_{C}-I_3)=8-2B+2\operatorname  {Re}\beta _{abc}$ with $B$ the modulus budget. Left: the two lines share the slope $2$ and differ only in $B$, so each crosses zero at $\operatorname  {Re}\beta _{abc}=B-4$: $\tfrac  75$ for the icosahedron, marked, and $1$ for the trine. A filled dot is a value some triple of the named design takes, an open dot an endpoint of the permitted range that it misses. The trine sits at the centre of its interval with $\operatorname  {Re}\beta _{abc}=0$, so its budget $5$ would have allowed $-2+2\sqrt  3>0$ and its phase does not, while the icosahedron is real and sits at both ends, ten of its triples landing above the zero level. Right: the disc $\lvert \beta _{abc}\rvert \le \sqrt  3$, the trine's eighteen mixed triples all on its boundary, the real locus its horizontal diameter, the shaded lens the region $\operatorname  {Re}\beta _{abc}>1$ where the determinant is positive, and the phase defect the squared height above the diameter, which is $3$ at $\pm i\sqrt  3$. Every abscissa drawn is a genuine triple of leverage-$3$ vectors, since the leading minors $3$, $9-\lvert \beta _{ab}\rvert ^{2}$ and $\det \operatorname  {Gram}_C=27-3B+2\operatorname  {Re}\beta _{abc}$ stay positive along both intervals, though the figure does not assert that each such triple sits inside a design; the icosahedral split of ten and ten is the one count \S  \ref {app:icosa} records without a theorem.}}{37}{figure.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:phasedefect}{{18}{37}{Identity \eqref {eq:tripledet} at $s=1$, where it reads $\det (\SC -I_3)=8-2B+2\operatorname {Re}\beta _{abc}$ with $B$ the modulus budget. Left: the two lines share the slope $2$ and differ only in $B$, so each crosses zero at $\operatorname {Re}\beta _{abc}=B-4$: $\tfrac 75$ for the icosahedron, marked, and $1$ for the trine. A filled dot is a value some triple of the named design takes, an open dot an endpoint of the permitted range that it misses. The trine sits at the centre of its interval with $\operatorname {Re}\beta _{abc}=0$, so its budget $5$ would have allowed $-2+2\sqrt 3>0$ and its phase does not, while the icosahedron is real and sits at both ends, ten of its triples landing above the zero level. Right: the disc $\lvert \beta _{abc}\rvert \le \sqrt 3$, the trine's eighteen mixed triples all on its boundary, the real locus its horizontal diameter, the shaded lens the region $\operatorname {Re}\beta _{abc}>1$ where the determinant is positive, and the phase defect the squared height above the diameter, which is $3$ at $\pm i\sqrt 3$. Every abscissa drawn is a genuine triple of leverage-$3$ vectors, since the leading minors $3$, $9-\lvert \beta _{ab}\rvert ^{2}$ and $\det \Gram _C=27-3B+2\operatorname {Re}\beta _{abc}$ stay positive along both intervals, though the figure does not assert that each such triple sits inside a design; the icosahedral split of ten and ten is the one count \S \ref {app:icosa} records without a theorem}{figure.18}{}}
