\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces The six constants of \S  \ref {sec:complex} on one axis. Below the axis, the ledger giving each its exact form and, for three of them, a polynomial it is the least root of: the floor $1/k$, the Hesse record $h_3$, the trine value $3-\sqrt  5$, the seed value $\alpha _{\mathrm  {SIC}}$, the padded bound $\tfrac  {10}9\alpha _{\mathrm  {SIC}}$, and the threshold $1$. Above it each rank carries the interval its two ends close, a filled dot marking an end attained by a design and an arrowhead an end running to $0$ with $1/k$. Rank two over $\mathbb  {C}$ is a point, while rank three spans $[\tfrac  13,h_3]$ and stays open: the light arrow on that row is the record of Remark\nonbreakingspace \ref {rem:sharpc} moving down through $\tfrac  {10}9\alpha _{\mathrm  {SIC}}$ and $3-\sqrt  5$ without pinning anything. The lower end that pins $\alpha _2(\mathbb  {C})$ is \cite  {Nesterenko2026complex} at uniform weights carried to arbitrary weights in \S  \ref {sec:cpadding}, and the top row's $\alpha _k(\mathbb  {R})=1$ is the conjecture; what is proved on that row is the bracket $[1/k,1]$ together with a design whose value is exactly $1$.}}{38}{figure.19}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:constants}{{19}{38}{The six constants of \S \ref {sec:complex} on one axis. Below the axis, the ledger giving each its exact form and, for three of them, a polynomial it is the least root of: the floor $1/k$, the Hesse record $h_3$, the trine value $3-\sqrt 5$, the seed value $\alpha _{\mathrm {SIC}}$, the padded bound $\tfrac {10}9\alpha _{\mathrm {SIC}}$, and the threshold $1$. Above it each rank carries the interval its two ends close, a filled dot marking an end attained by a design and an arrowhead an end running to $0$ with $1/k$. Rank two over $\C $ is a point, while rank three spans $[\tfrac 13,h_3]$ and stays open: the light arrow on that row is the record of Remark~\ref {rem:sharpc} moving down through $\tfrac {10}9\alpha _{\mathrm {SIC}}$ and $3-\sqrt 5$ without pinning anything. The lower end that pins $\alpha _2(\C )$ is \cite {Nesterenko2026complex} at uniform weights carried to arbitrary weights in \S \ref {sec:cpadding}, and the top row's $\alpha _k(\R )=1$ is the conjecture; what is proved on that row is the bracket $[1/k,1]$ together with a design whose value is exactly $1$}{figure.19}{}}
