\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces The split tetrahedron at $(7,3)$ of Example\nonbreakingspace \ref {ex:split}, and all thirty-five of its triples on one eigenvalue axis. Top left: the seven atoms by direction class, three classes of two and one of one, each carrying total weight $\tfrac  14$; the count on the right runs over the resulting multiplicities $(2,2,2,1)$. Bottom left: both spectra on one axis with the threshold dashed, the twenty triples that meet three directions on the upper row and the fifteen that repeat one below, singular on the line orthogonal to the two directions they carry. Right: the multipliers of the stationarity datum of Example\nonbreakingspace \ref {ex:splitseven}, positive and summing to one; they are also the volume-sampling probabilities \eqref  {eq:dpp} of the same triples, $\pi _C=\bigl  (\DOTSB \prod@ \slimits@ _{c\in C}t_c\bigr  )\cdot 16$. The lower spectrum is computed here, and of the three symmetric functions behind it the development carries the first and the last.}}{53}{figure.26}\protected@file@percent }
