\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces The certificate of the star at $(5,3)$, and the two circuits. Left: the zero sets of the two linear forms of \eqref  {eq:diamondstar}, drawn in the potential space; $Q_C$ vanishes where both do, on their common line, and congruence by $R$ carries that inertia to $S_{C}-I_3$. The witness $(1,4,0)$ of Example\nonbreakingspace \ref {ex:diamond} lies on that line, and the tally below the panel is \eqref  {eq:diamondgap} evaluated there. Right: the two circuits $\{e_0,e_1,e_3\}$ and $\{e_0,e_2,e_4\}$, each shaded on its vertex set; a potential constant there kills the three selected drops and leaves the two edges of the cut, of conductance $3$ and drop $1$ apiece. That eight of the ten $3$-subsets dominate, each with a one-dimensional kernel and the eight null lines pairwise distinct, is computed here; Example\nonbreakingspace \ref {ex:diamond} needs one dominating subset and the ten nonpositive values, and the development proves no more.}}{57}{figure.29}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:diamondcert}{{29}{57}{The certificate of the star at $(5,3)$, and the two circuits. Left: the zero sets of the two linear forms of \eqref {eq:diamondstar}, drawn in the potential space; $Q_C$ vanishes where both do, on their common line, and congruence by $R$ carries that inertia to $\SC -I_3$. The witness $(1,4,0)$ of Example~\ref {ex:diamond} lies on that line, and the tally below the panel is \eqref {eq:diamondgap} evaluated there. Right: the two circuits $\{e_0,e_1,e_3\}$ and $\{e_0,e_2,e_4\}$, each shaded on its vertex set; a potential constant there kills the three selected drops and leaves the two edges of the cut, of conductance $3$ and drop $1$ apiece. That eight of the ten $3$-subsets dominate, each with a one-dimensional kernel and the eight null lines pairwise distinct, is computed here; Example~\ref {ex:diamond} needs one dominating subset and the ten nonpositive values, and the development proves no more}{figure.29}{}}
