\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {36}{\ignorespaces The spectraplex \eqref  {eq:spectraplex} at $k=2$. Trace one cuts the cone in a horizontal disc of radius $\tfrac  12$, whose boundary circle meets each extreme ray once and consists of the rank-one projectors. Since $\lambda _{\min }$ is concave, \eqref  {eq:spectraplex} is a superdifferential; it is the face of that disc spanned by the unit eigenvectors at $\lambda _{\min }(A)$. The face at an eigenspace of dimension $r$ has dimension $\tfrac  12r(r+1)-1$, so none has dimension one, and the multiplier attached to an active subset in Theorem\nonbreakingspace \ref {thm:firstorder} is a positive semidefinite matrix of trace one on the eigenspace rather than a scalar. Neither the spectraplex nor the passage of \S  \ref {sec:kkt} from a local minimum to \eqref  {eq:statg}--\eqref  {eq:statt} is formal (\S  \ref {sec:boundaries}).}}{75}{figure.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spectraplex}{{36}{75}{The spectraplex \eqref {eq:spectraplex} at $k=2$. Trace one cuts the cone in a horizontal disc of radius $\tfrac 12$, whose boundary circle meets each extreme ray once and consists of the rank-one projectors. Since $\lmin $ is concave, \eqref {eq:spectraplex} is a superdifferential; it is the face of that disc spanned by the unit eigenvectors at $\lmin (A)$. The face at an eigenspace of dimension $r$ has dimension $\tfrac 12r(r+1)-1$, so none has dimension one, and the multiplier attached to an active subset in Theorem~\ref {thm:firstorder} is a positive semidefinite matrix of trace one on the eigenspace rather than a scalar. Neither the spectraplex nor the passage of \S \ref {sec:kkt} from a local minimum to \eqref {eq:statg}--\eqref {eq:statt} is formal (\S \ref {sec:boundaries})}{figure.36}{}}
