\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {42}{\ignorespaces Bounded-radius exchange at $(6,3)$. Left: the twenty triples by exchange distance from the stuck $\{0,1,2\}$, in shells of $1$, $9$, $9$ and $1$, with the ball of radius two shaded; the heavy path is the geodesic $\{0,1,2\}$, $013$, $034$, $\{3,4,5\}$, one swap at each step. Right: the design, and the score axis, on which the hatched band is a ceiling over the eighteen and not their values, which run off to its left. The stuck triple scores $576/625$, which is $49/625$ below the threshold, and the eighteen others within two swaps score at most $512/625$, which is $64/625$ below the stuck one; the only triple that dominates scores $4$ and lies three swaps away, three being the rank. So radius $k$ is global search and radius $k-1$ cannot see the subset it needs.}}{103}{figure.42}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:exchange}{{42}{103}{Bounded-radius exchange at $(6,3)$. Left: the twenty triples by exchange distance from the stuck $\{0,1,2\}$, in shells of $1$, $9$, $9$ and $1$, with the ball of radius two shaded; the heavy path is the geodesic $\{0,1,2\}$, $013$, $034$, $\{3,4,5\}$, one swap at each step. Right: the design, and the score axis, on which the hatched band is a ceiling over the eighteen and not their values, which run off to its left. The stuck triple scores $576/625$, which is $49/625$ below the threshold, and the eighteen others within two swaps score at most $512/625$, which is $64/625$ below the stuck one; the only triple that dominates scores $4$ and lies three swaps away, three being the rank. So radius $k$ is global search and radius $k-1$ cannot see the subset it needs}{figure.42}{}}
