\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {45}{\ignorespaces The twenty triples of the icosahedral design. Left: the sign of $\langle g_c,g_d\rangle /\rho $ at each pair, the diagonal carrying the leverage $3$. Middle: the triples sorted by the product $\tau $ of their three pairings. Right: the trace $9$, the pairing squares $27/5$ and the modulus $\rho ^3$ are common to all twenty, and every line below them is a function of the sign of $\tau $; the two spectra are $\{3-\rho ,3-\rho ,3+2\rho \}$ and $\{3-2\rho ,3+\rho ,3+\rho \}$, and no other occurs. The split ten and ten is the assertion with no theorem behind it; the criterion that produces it, that the product of the three pairings normalized by the excesses $\ell _c-1$ be at least $7/40$, is kernel-checked.}}{111}{figure.45}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:icosaspec}{{45}{111}{The twenty triples of the icosahedral design. Left: the sign of $\langle g_c,g_d\rangle /\rho $ at each pair, the diagonal carrying the leverage $3$. Middle: the triples sorted by the product $\tau $ of their three pairings. Right: the trace $9$, the pairing squares $27/5$ and the modulus $\rho ^3$ are common to all twenty, and every line below them is a function of the sign of $\tau $; the two spectra are $\{3-\rho ,3-\rho ,3+2\rho \}$ and $\{3-2\rho ,3+\rho ,3+\rho \}$, and no other occurs. The split ten and ten is the assertion with no theorem behind it; the criterion that produces it, that the product of the three pairings normalized by the excesses $\ell _c-1$ be at least $7/40$, is kernel-checked}{figure.45}{}}
